  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Wildlife Without Borders-Western Hemisphere</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project seeks to equip peasant farmers from all over Mexico with the knowledge and skills necessary to sustainably harvest wild agave plants (Agave cupreata) as a non-timber forest product while generating an alternative and sustainable source of income for their families. Through this grant the proponent will: 1) organize a national meeting for agave growers and mezcal producers to exchange experiences and agree on common conservation and sustainable management strategies of the species; 2) implement 2 workshops on monitoring, data analysis, and sustainable production of wild agave plants for peasant farmers in Guerrero; 3) perform 2 exchange of experiences visits among the mezcal producing communities of the states of Guerrero and Jalisco; 4) perform community participatory data collection and monitoring activities of flora and fauna in the Acateyahualco Region of Guerrero; 5) perform a community participatory diagnostic and develop best management practices to diminish the impact of cattle grazing in the populations of wild agave plants; 6) organize a participatory diagnostic workshop on the management , use and extraction of wild agave plants with representatives of 16 mezcal producing communities from La Joya Region of Guerrero; and 7) build 3 demonstrative parcels to determine the best harvesting and restoration techniques for agave plants.</narrative>
  </description>
